WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.106
Lesson 2.4: Evaluating AI Feedback and Ethics in Theater.

2
00:00:06.106 --> 00:00:09.320
GENAI 101 - Module 2: Theater.

3
00:00:09.320 --> 00:00:15.747
Presented by Idowu David Awoyemi, The University of Alabama.

4
00:00:16.347 --> 00:00:22.670
Think of a piece of feedback on your creative work that you
rejected and were right to reject.

5
00:00:22.670 --> 00:00:23.948
What made you sure?

6
00:00:23.948 --> 00:00:35.854
Usually one of three things: the note misread the work, the
note was so general it could apply to anything, or the note
asked for a different piece than the one you were making.

7
00:00:35.854 --> 00:00:39.553
You'll use those same three tests on AI feedback today.

8
00:00:40.153 --> 00:00:46.391
A text model will produce feedback on anything you paste,
instantly and confidently.

9
00:00:46.391 --> 00:00:53.667
It has no stake in your scene, no memory of your rehearsal
room, and no way to know your students.

10
00:00:53.667 --> 00:00:59.162
Its feedback is a statistical average of what feedback
usually looks like.

11
00:00:59.162 --> 00:01:05.251
That average can be genuinely useful for spotting structural
problems and cliches.

12
00:01:05.251 --> 00:01:11.043
It is useless, or harmful, when it pushes a distinctive
voice toward the mean.

13
00:01:11.643 --> 00:01:15.188
Classify every note into one of three classes.

14
00:01:15.188 --> 00:01:25.822
Useful feedback points to a specific line, names a problem
you can verify by rereading, and suggests a change
consistent with your intent.

15
00:01:25.822 --> 00:01:33.991
Generic feedback could be said about any scene: raise the
stakes, develop the characters, show don't tell.

16
00:01:33.991 --> 00:01:38.924
Ask the model to locate it in the text; if it can't, discard
it.

17
00:01:38.924 --> 00:01:46.245
Wrong feedback misreads the text, invents a detail, or
pushes the scene toward a different one.

18
00:01:46.245 --> 00:01:51.331
You'll classify five notes and cite the text as evidence for
each.

19
00:01:51.931 --> 00:01:54.993
Feedback quality depends on the request.

20
00:01:54.993 --> 00:01:58.437
Specify the lens, the criteria, and the form.

21
00:01:58.437 --> 00:02:04.713
Then ask a follow-up: which of these notes are you least
confident about, and why?

22
00:02:04.713 --> 00:02:09.842
Models are often more honest about uncertainty when asked
directly.

23
00:02:09.842 --> 00:02:16.807
Test one useful note in a rewrite and keep both versions;
you'll post the before and after.

24
00:02:17.407 --> 00:02:22.908
Finally, the ethics of putting AI-assisted work in front of
an audience.

25
00:02:22.908 --> 00:02:24.054
Five questions.

26
00:02:24.054 --> 00:02:29.861
Authorship: who is credited when a model drafted forty
percent of the lines?

27
00:02:29.861 --> 00:02:40.175
Consent: did the writers whose plays trained the model
agree, and did your students agree to have their
improvisations fed into a tool?

28
00:02:40.175 --> 00:02:52.095
Representation: models reproduce the biases of their
training data, and AI-generated characters from
underrepresented communities can default to stereotype.

29
00:02:52.095 --> 00:02:54.922
Labor: which jobs are you automating?

30
00:02:54.922 --> 00:02:58.895
Disclosure: does the audience know, and should they?

31
00:02:58.895 --> 00:03:01.340
There is no single right answer.

32
00:03:01.340 --> 00:03:04.014
There is one wrong one: not asking.

33
00:03:04.014 --> 00:03:11.578
Write your position, print the credit line you'd put in the
program, and argue it out in the forum.
